      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Integrating ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$ from ${t}_{0}$ to $t$ in $\\text{L3}$ yields\n\n$$\ny(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by $\\text{integrating both sides from }{t}_{0}\\text{ to }t.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Evaluating $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$ at $t={t}_{0}$ gives\n\n$$\ny({t}_{0})={e}^{-A({t}_{0}-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})=Ix({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{evaluating at }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L1}$ by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t) \\\\  & =x(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by }{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}.$",
